1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,267
This is ECE 358, video 4 a. The topics are protocol layers and encapsulation, with question bank questions 44 to 51.

2
00:00:12,717 --> 00:00:34,349
After this video, you can name the 5 Internet layers, with their jobs and protocols. You can explain encapsulation. You can calculate the header overhead of a frame. And you can answer questions 44 to 51. Video 4 b then solves the long question bank problem about a path of four routers.

3
00:00:34,799 --> 00:00:43,993
Chapter 1 showed many parts of a network. There are hosts, routers, and links. The picture shows a small network with all of these parts.

4
00:00:44,443 --> 00:00:56,753
The slide lists the pieces. Hosts, routers, links of various media, applications, protocols, and hardware and software. A real network has millions of these pieces.

5
00:00:57,203 --> 00:01:06,013
So the slide asks a question. How can we organize the structure of the network? And how can we organize our discussion of networks?

6
00:01:06,463 --> 00:01:11,967
The answer is layers. The slides explain layers with an example from air travel.

7
00:01:12,417 --> 00:01:25,943
Think of a trip by airplane. At the departure airport, you do a series of steps. You purchase a ticket. You check your baggage. You go through the gate. The airplane takes off. Then airplane routing starts.

8
00:01:26,393 --> 00:01:29,913
Between the two airports, the airplane follows its route.

9
00:01:30,363 --> 00:01:42,245
At the arrival airport, the steps occur in the opposite order. Airplane routing, runway landing, gates unload, baggage claim. Last, you can complain about the ticket.

10
00:01:42,695 --> 00:01:52,295
The full system gives one end-to-end service. It moves a person plus baggage. It is a series of steps, and it involves many services.

11
00:01:52,745 --> 00:02:09,812
Now put the matching steps in one row. Ticket purchase and ticket complaint form the ticketing service. Baggage check and baggage claim form the baggage service. Then come the gate service, the runway service, and the routing service. Each row is one layer.

12
00:02:10,262 --> 00:02:22,998
Look at the baggage layer. It has its own internal actions. For example, the airline puts a tag on the bag, and reads the tag at arrival. The passenger does not see these actions.

13
00:02:23,448 --> 00:02:35,821
The baggage layer relies on the layer below. The gate service loads the bag into the airplane, and unloads it. The baggage layer does not need to know how the runway or the routing work.

14
00:02:36,271 --> 00:02:47,813
This gives the definition of a layer. Each layer implements a service. It uses its own internal-layer actions. And it relies on the services of the layer below.

15
00:02:48,263 --> 00:03:05,180
Why do we use layers? First, layering gives an explicit structure. It identifies the pieces of the system and their relationships. Each layer gives a service to the layer above. The layered reference model is also a common base for discussion.

16
00:03:05,630 --> 00:03:25,342
Second, layering is modularization. It supports 3 key attributes of large distributed systems. The first attribute is seamless evolution. For example, IPv6 can replace IPv4 in the network layer. The other layers do not change.

17
00:03:25,792 --> 00:03:35,243
The second attribute is ease of scalability. For example, a new tier 1 network can join the Internet. It uses the same layers.

18
00:03:35,693 --> 00:03:49,815
The third attribute is easy maintenance and updating. For example, you can remove old links and switches and add new ones. This changes the physical layer, but the transport layer needs no change.

19
00:03:50,265 --> 00:04:00,761
The rule: a change inside one layer is transparent to the rest of the system. This is true when the service of that layer to the layer above stays the same.

20
00:04:01,211 --> 00:04:21,926
The Internet has 5 layers. The top layer is the application layer. It supports network applications. Examples are HTTP, I MAP, S M T P and DNS. The picture shows host A, a router R, and host B. The application on A talks to the application on B.

21
00:04:22,376 --> 00:04:37,224
Below it is the transport layer. It transfers data from one process to another process. Examples are TCP and UDP. Its reach is process to process, from a program on A to a program on B.

22
00:04:37,674 --> 00:04:53,717
Next is the network layer. It routes datagrams, also called IP packets, from the source machine to the destination machine. Examples are IP and the routing protocols. Its reach is host to host, through the routers.

23
00:04:54,167 --> 00:05:09,356
Next is the link layer. It transfers data between neighboring network elements, such as hosts and switches. Examples are Ethernet, 802.11 WiFi, and PPP. Its reach is one link only.

24
00:05:09,806 --> 00:05:19,193
The bottom layer is the physical layer. It puts the bits on the wire. From bottom to top, each layer extends the reach of the layer below it.

25
00:05:19,643 --> 00:05:33,083
Now follow data down the layers of the source host. The application layer exchanges messages. We call the message M. To send it, the application uses the service of the transport layer.

26
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:58,045
The transport layer encapsulates M. It puts the transport-layer header, H t, in front of M. The result is a segment. H t carries the control information of the transport layer. Examples are the sequence number, the ACK, and an error checking code. The transport protocol uses H t to give its service, for example reliable data transfer.

27
00:05:58,495 --> 00:06:18,761
The network layer encapsulates the segment. It puts the network-layer header, H n, in front. The result is a datagram, also called an IP packet. H n carries the source IP address, the destination IP address, an error checking code, and the time to live, TTL.

28
00:06:19,211 --> 00:06:32,801
The link layer encapsulates the datagram. It puts the link-layer header, H l, in front. The result is a frame. The link layer moves the frame to the neighboring device, with the physical layer.

29
00:06:33,251 --> 00:06:47,907
This is the principle of encapsulation. Each layer takes the unit from the layer above. It puts its own header in front. Then it gives the result to the layer below. The layer does not change the unit from above.

30
00:06:48,357 --> 00:06:59,727
Here is the complete picture. At the source, the data goes down the stack. Each layer adds a header. The names of the units are message, segment, datagram, and frame.

31
00:07:00,177 --> 00:07:03,996
The physical layer sends the bits of the frame over the medium.

32
00:07:04,446 --> 00:07:17,161
At the destination, the data goes up the stack. Each layer reads its own header and removes it. Then it gives the rest to the layer above. The application gets the original message M.

33
00:07:17,611 --> 00:07:45,067
Take a message of 100 bytes as an example. Use the header sizes of slide 63: 20 bytes for TCP, 20 bytes for IP, and 14 bytes for Ethernet. The segment is 120 bytes. The datagram is 140 bytes. The frame is 154 bytes. At the destination, the sizes go back down: 140, 120, and 100 bytes.

34
00:07:45,517 --> 00:07:58,637
Each header is for the same layer on the other side. The headers go on in the order transport, network, link. So the destination removes them in the opposite order: link, network, transport.

35
00:07:59,087 --> 00:08:07,279
Slide 62 adds the devices between the two hosts. The source host makes the frame. It has all 5 layers.

36
00:08:07,729 --> 00:08:26,097
First the frame reaches a link-layer switch. A switch has only two layers: link and physical. It reads only the link header, H l. Then it sends the frame on the next link. The switch is transparent to the source host and to the router. They do not know that it is there.

37
00:08:26,547 --> 00:08:45,597
Next the frame reaches a router. A router has three layers: network, link, and physical. It removes H l and reads H n, for example the destination IP address. It selects the next link. Then it puts a new H l in front of the datagram, and sends the frame.

38
00:08:46,047 --> 00:08:54,239
At the destination host, the data goes up all 5 layers. Each layer removes its header. The application gets M.

39
00:08:54,689 --> 00:09:07,852
So a host uses 5 layers. A router uses 3 layers and reads the network header. A switch uses 2 layers and reads the link header. No router or switch reads H t or M.

40
00:09:08,302 --> 00:09:22,510
Slide 63 gives the advantages of encapsulation. First, the layers are modular. Each layer touches only its own header. So a change in one layer does not need a change in another layer.

41
00:09:22,960 --> 00:09:35,333
Second, encapsulation protects the data of the upper layers. A layer can put an error checking code in its header. The receiving layer uses it to find bit errors in the data from above.

42
00:09:35,783 --> 00:09:54,941
The disadvantage is overhead. Every header must also be transmitted, but it does not carry application data. The slide gives the header lengths. The TCP header is 20 bytes or more. The IP header is 20 bytes or more. The Ethernet header is 14 bytes or more.

43
00:09:55,391 --> 00:10:09,001
Draw the 154-byte frame of our example to scale. The three headers use 14 plus 20 plus 20, which is 54 bytes. The message uses the other 100 bytes.

44
00:10:09,451 --> 00:10:23,958
The overhead fraction is the header bytes divided by the frame bytes. Here, 54 divided by 154 is 35.1 percent. So more than one third of this frame is overhead.

45
00:10:24,408 --> 00:10:46,659
Here is the method to calculate the encapsulation overhead. Add the header lengths: 20 plus 20 plus 14 is 54 bytes per frame. Then divide by the frame length, which is the message length plus 54. The orange curve shows this fraction for message sizes up to 1500 bytes.

46
00:10:47,109 --> 00:10:55,151
A 100-byte message gives 54 divided by 154. That is 35.1 percent.

47
00:10:55,601 --> 00:11:11,836
A 946-byte message fills a 1000-byte frame. Its overhead is 54 divided by 1000, which is only 5.4 percent. We meet this frame again in the long question of video 4 b.

48
00:11:12,286 --> 00:11:24,553
The header cost is fixed for each frame. Small messages pay the same 54 bytes. So the overhead fraction is high for small messages, and low for large messages.

49
00:11:25,003 --> 00:11:39,296
Now the question bank. Questions 44 and 47 ask how many layers the Internet model has, and for one example protocol in each layer, from link to application. Pause the video and try it.

50
00:11:39,746 --> 00:11:47,767
Count the layers on slide 57. Application, transport, network, link, and physical. There are 5 layers.

51
00:11:48,217 --> 00:11:55,940
Application layer: HTTP. Other examples are I MAP, S M T P and DNS.

52
00:11:56,390 --> 00:12:00,955
Transport layer: TCP. Another example is UDP.

53
00:12:01,405 --> 00:12:06,419
Network layer: IP. The routing protocols are also in this layer.

54
00:12:06,869 --> 00:12:25,279
Link layer: Ethernet. Other examples are 802.11 WiFi and PPP. The physical layer puts the bits on the wire. For the answer, one protocol per layer is enough. For example HTTP, TCP, IP, and Ethernet.

55
00:12:25,729 --> 00:12:32,663
Question 45. Why is the Internet software organized as layers? Pause the video and try it.

56
00:12:33,113 --> 00:12:46,147
Use slide 56. First reason: layering gives an explicit structure. It identifies the pieces of the system and their relationships. It also gives a reference model for discussion.

57
00:12:46,597 --> 00:13:05,691
Second reason: layering is modularization. It supports seamless evolution, such as IPv4 to IPv6. It supports ease of scalability, such as a new tier 1 network. And it supports easy maintenance, such as new links and switches.

58
00:13:06,141 --> 00:13:18,407
So a good answer says this. Layering gives an explicit structure, and it supports evolution, scalability, and maintenance. A change in one layer is transparent to the other layers.

59
00:13:18,857 --> 00:13:24,511
Question 46. Explain the function of each layer. Pause the video and try it.

60
00:13:24,961 --> 00:13:32,513
Use slide 57. Application: it supports network applications, for example HTTP.

61
00:13:32,963 --> 00:13:39,683
Transport: it transfers data from one process to another process, for example TCP.

62
00:13:40,133 --> 00:13:47,343
Network: it routes datagrams from the source machine to the destination machine. The protocol is IP.

63
00:13:47,793 --> 00:13:56,284
Link: it transfers data between neighboring network elements, for example Ethernet. Physical: it puts the bits on the wire.

64
00:13:56,734 --> 00:14:05,609
Questions 48 and 49. Explain the principle of encapsulation, and give its advantages. Pause the video and try it.

65
00:14:06,059 --> 00:14:21,888
The principle: each layer takes the unit from the layer above. It puts its own header in front. Then it gives the result to the layer below. The picture shows the network layer: it puts H n in front of the segment and makes a datagram.

66
00:14:22,338 --> 00:14:31,810
The header carries the control information of that layer. The same layer at the receiver reads the header, removes it, and passes the rest up.

67
00:14:32,260 --> 00:14:47,748
The advantages are on slide 63. First, the layers are modular, so a change in one layer needs no change in another layer. Second, the layer-specific error checking codes protect the data of the upper layers.

68
00:14:48,198 --> 00:14:59,291
Questions 50 and 51. How is the encapsulation overhead calculated? And what are the key words for the unit of each layer? Pause the video and try it.

69
00:14:59,741 --> 00:15:14,760
For question 50, add the header lengths of all layers. With the minimum sizes, that is 20 plus 20 plus 14, which is 54 bytes. The overhead fraction is the header bytes divided by the frame length.

70
00:15:15,210 --> 00:15:20,885
If the frame length is fixed, the message bytes are the frame length minus the header bytes.

71
00:15:21,335 --> 00:15:28,076
For question 51, use slide 61. The application layer exchanges messages.

72
00:15:28,526 --> 00:15:31,129
The transport layer exchanges segments.

73
00:15:31,579 --> 00:15:36,357
The network layer exchanges datagrams, also called IP packets.

74
00:15:36,807 --> 00:15:43,869
And the link layer exchanges frames. Each unit is the unit of the layer above, plus one more header.

75
00:15:44,319 --> 00:15:53,727
Here is the recap. One. A layer implements a service with its own internal actions, and relies on the service of the layer below.

76
00:15:54,177 --> 00:16:04,865
Two. Layering gives an explicit structure. It supports evolution, scalability, and maintenance. A change in one layer is transparent to the other layers.

77
00:16:05,315 --> 00:16:14,893
Three. The Internet has 5 layers. Application, transport, network, link, and physical. Know one protocol and the reach of each layer.

78
00:16:15,343 --> 00:16:27,034
Four. In encapsulation, each layer adds its own header. The units are message, segment, datagram, and frame. The receiver removes the headers in the opposite order.

79
00:16:27,484 --> 00:16:33,265
Five. A host uses 5 layers. A router uses 3, and a switch uses 2.

80
00:16:33,715 --> 00:16:48,649
Six. The overhead is the sum of the headers, 54 bytes with the minimum sizes. Divide by the frame length to get the overhead fraction. The next video, 4 b, solves the long Chapter 1 question.
